# =============================================================================
# air-aspect-admit — admitted AIR proof-aspect shapes.
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • the aspect report is lane-agnostic: a literal `itera ab` range carries
#     concrete lane facts, so the report records an UnrollEquivalence
#     obligation (machine-checked-structural evidence class — no
#     semantic-preservation or numeric claim)
#   • a body of immutable bindings ending in `return <path>` sits inside the
#     v1 scalar/let/call subset, so the report records a GatePurity obligation
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • reading an admitted record as a proven theorem — recognition carries the
#     obligation; proof verdicts come from the Lean checker
#   • routing these shapes through an `@ radix lane "air"` annotation when the
#     target backend drops AIR-lane functions — the report sees the checked
#     HIR of ordinary declarations
#
# See also: itera, ab, air-aspect-decline
# =============================================================================

# Declaration-only proof fixture: the aspect report inspects these functions;
# this source intentionally has no executable entry point.
#
# Admitted Line 1 shape: literal range gives concrete lanes (route ab, 4 lanes).
fn unroll_admit() → void {
    for range 0‥4 const i {
    }
}

# Admitted Line 3 shape: immutable let chain + returned path — inside the
# scalar/let/call purity subset, no IO or monadic effect forms.
fn purum(int x) → int {
    let int s ← x
    return s
}
